GNU tar for OpenVMS
===================

This is GNU tar @VERSION@ built natively for OpenVMS @ARCH@, from the
unmodified release plus the patches in the vms-tar project:

    https://github.com/issinoho/vms-tar

Kit version: @KITVERSION@.  GNU tar is free software under the GNU General
Public License, version 3 or later; see COPYING.  The manual page is
TAR.1, also as plain text in TAR.TXT.

What is installed
-----------------
    TAR$ROOT:[BIN]TAR.EXE
    TAR$ROOT:[DOC]                    this file, COPYING, NEWS, the manual
    TAR$ROOT:[000000]TAR$SETUP.COM    defines the tar command
    SYS$STARTUP:TAR$STARTUP.COM       defines TAR$ROOT

Setting up
----------
The kit installs TAR$STARTUP.COM into SYS$STARTUP and runs it once.  To
define TAR$ROOT at every boot, the system manager adds this line to
SYS$MANAGER:SYSTARTUP_VMS.COM:

    $ @SYS$STARTUP:TAR$STARTUP.COM

Each user (or SYLOGIN.COM, for everyone) then defines the command:

    $ @TAR$ROOT:[000000]TAR$SETUP.COM

Using it
--------
    $ tar "-cf" backup.tar [.dir]     ! create an archive
    $ tar "-tvf" backup.tar           ! list it
    $ tar "-xf" backup.tar            ! extract it

Compressed archives (-z, -j, -J, --zstd) run the gzip, bzip2, xz and zstd
commands from the vms-gzip, vms-bzip2, vms-xz and vms-zstd kits.  The
archive passes through a temporary file in SYS$SCRATCH, so a compressed
archive cannot be standard input or output.

File names
----------
Files and directories can be named in either syntax.  tar converts VMS
names to Unix names, so the archive's member names are the same either way
and any tar can read them:

    [.dir]  dir  dir.dir         -> member dir/ and everything below it
    [.dir.sub]file.txt;1         -> member dir/sub/file.txt

This holds for names on the command line, in -T lists, after -C, and for
members to extract.  The archive name after -f may be in either syntax.

Record files
------------
Text files with VMS record formats (variable-length, VFC, or with
carriage control) are archived as their lines, each ending in a newline,
with exact member sizes.  Extracted files are Stream_LF; a binary's
original record format (for example fixed 512) is not restored, so set it
again with SET FILE/ATTRIBUTES if the program that reads it needs it.

Not available on OpenVMS
------------------------
Remote archives (host:file, through rsh), --to-command, --info-script and
--checkpoint-action=exec.  A name with a colon is a local VMS device name.

Exit status
-----------
Under DCL a failed run exits with an error-severity status, so ON ERROR and
IF .NOT. $STATUS work; the exit code N is ($STATUS .AND. %X7F8) / 8.  Exit
code 1 (some files differ, or changed while being archived) has warning
severity.  Under a GNV shell, $? is the exit code as on Unix.

Upper-case options and batch jobs
---------------------------------
Under the TRADITIONAL DCL parse style DCL upper-cases the unquoted parts of
a command and the C run-time library passes them to the program in lower
case.  Batch jobs use TRADITIONAL even when interactive logins are set to
EXTENDED.  Quote options with capitals (such as "-C" and "-T"), use the
long forms, or start the procedure with

    $ SET PROCESS/PARSE_STYLE=EXTENDED
